X('how-to-start-an-import-export-business-in-india',[
 ('The registrations in order',['PAN and a business entity: a proprietorship, partnership, LLP or company, depending on size and risk.','GST registration, which is needed to claim input credit and to export under a letter of undertaking.','The importer-exporter code from DGFT, issued online against your PAN and bank details.','A current account with a bank that handles foreign exchange, and registration of the bank as your authorised dealer with customs.','An RCMC from an export promotion council if you want to claim export benefits.']),
 ('Choosing a product and a buyer',['Pick a product whose HS code, duty and policy you have checked. Look at import policy and conditions for imports and the rules of the destination for exports. Use the duty calculator and our guides to get a first view, then ask a broker to confirm.','Verify the other party. Ask for company registration, a bank reference and trade references, and start with smaller orders and safer payment terms such as a letter of credit.']),
 ('Common first-year mistakes',['Agreeing a price without working out the freight, duty and clearance charges.','Paying a large advance to an unknown supplier with no protection.','Ignoring the paperwork until the cargo is at the port. Talk to your broker before the order is placed, not after.'])],
 [('How long does it take to get an IEC?','Usually a short time once the application is made online with the PAN and the bank details, but it depends on the portal and your documents. Plan for a few days.')])
X('ecgc-export-credit-insurance',[
 ('What kinds of risk it covers',['Commercial risks, such as the buyer becoming insolvent or failing to pay for a long period after the due date. Political risks, such as war, a ban on transfers of foreign exchange or a cancelled import licence in the buyer\'s country. The cover does not pay for losses caused by the exporter\'s own faults, a dispute about the quality of the goods or a breach of the sales contract.','The policy pays a percentage of the loss and not the whole amount, so the exporter keeps some of the risk.']),
 ('How to use it',['Choose a policy that matches your trade, such as one for a single shipment, for all shipments or for small exporters. Ask ECGC or your bank which policies suit the way you sell.','Report the shipments and any overdue payments within the time the policy requires, because late reporting can reduce a claim.','Check the buyer limits. The insurer sets a limit for each buyer, and cover applies only up to that limit.']),
 ('Banks and financing',['Banks often prefer exporters who hold cover, and some give better terms for financing when the exports are insured. Ask your bank about this, since the cover can improve access to credit as well as protect you from a loss.'])],
 [('Does ECGC pay if my buyer disputes the quality?','Generally no. A dispute about the goods or the contract is not a covered risk. Cover is for non-payment because of the buyer\'s insolvency, default or a political event.')])
X('sez-and-eou-explained',[
 ('How the benefits work',['A special economic zone is a notified area that is treated as outside the normal customs territory for trade. Units in the zone can import goods for their authorised operations without paying duty and can receive supplies from the rest of India without GST, under conditions. Their sales to the rest of India are treated like imports and pay duty. An export oriented unit is a factory outside a zone that works under a bond and must export most of its output.','Both schemes are tied to export performance, and the units must meet obligations.']),
 ('Moving cargo in practice',['Goods moving into an SEZ are handled by the zone\'s customs officers, with documents that differ from a normal bill of entry. Goods leaving for export go through the zone procedure and the shipping bill is filed there. Supplies from the domestic area to a zone are treated as exports for GST and for some benefits, and have to show the proof of receipt.']),
 ('Compliance and risk',['Keep accurate stock records and the monthly or yearly returns that the scheme needs. A mismatch between stock and records can lead to a demand for duty. Check the current rules and any changes in the law, since the schemes have been updated several times.'])],
 [('Can an SEZ unit sell in the Indian market?','Yes, but the sale is treated as an import into India, and duty and taxes are charged as for any import, under the rules and conditions.')])
X('merchanting-trade-explained',[
 ('A typical deal',['An Indian company buys steel from a supplier in Country A and sells it to a customer in Country B. The goods go from A to B directly, and never enter India. The company earns the margin between the buying and selling prices. The transaction does not appear on an Indian customs bill, but it is regulated by the central bank.','Because the goods never reach India, the Indian company has no customs documents to rely on for proof. Its bank and the trade documents become the record.']),
 ('Conditions to expect',['The central bank sets conditions, which include a time limit to complete the deal, a limit on the amount outstanding and the requirement that the goods are not restricted. The details are in the current directions, so ask your bank for the present rules.','The bank will want to see the purchase and sale contracts, the shipping documents and evidence that the goods moved from the first country to the second.']),
 ('Risks',['Payment risk on both sides: you may pay the supplier before you are paid by the buyer. Use a letter of credit or another secure term for the buying and selling sides.','Compliance risk: sanctions, restricted goods and the origin of the goods in the first country. Check each party and the goods before you commit.','Timing risk: the deal must close within the allowed period, so delays can create a violation.'])],
 [('Is merchanting trade a customs matter in India?','Not directly, since the goods do not enter India. It is mainly a foreign exchange matter handled through your bank under the central bank rules.')])
X('trade-finance-options-for-exporters',[
 ('Before the shipment',['Packing credit lets an exporter borrow against a confirmed order or a letter of credit to buy raw material, make the goods and pack them. It is given in rupees or in a foreign currency and is repaid from the export proceeds. The bank looks at the order, the buyer and the exporter\'s record, and may ask for credit insurance.']),
 ('After the shipment',['Once the goods are shipped, the bank can buy or discount the export bill, giving the exporter the money earlier than the buyer pays. Factoring and forfaiting are other ways to turn a receivable into cash, usually for larger or longer-term sales, and are often done without recourse to the exporter. Each has a cost, which is deducted from the amount received.']),
 ('What banks will ask for',['Your IEC, GST and PAN, and your financial statements for the last years.','The export order, the letter of credit or the contract with the buyer.','Information about the buyer and the country, and proof of the shipping documents.','Evidence of credit insurance, if it is part of the terms.'])],
 [('What is the difference between pre-shipment and post-shipment finance?','Pre-shipment finance is given before the goods ship, to produce and pack them. Post-shipment finance is given after shipment against the export documents, until the buyer pays.')])
X('dedicated-freight-corridor-containers',[
 ('The routes',['The western corridor runs from the Mumbai area to the Delhi region, with the container terminal at Dadri on the Delhi side, and links to the Gujarat ports. The eastern corridor runs from Punjab towards Kolkata. The corridors have separate tracks for freight so that goods trains do not compete with passenger trains, and they are built for heavier loads and double-stack container trains. Sections have opened in stages, so check which parts are working on your route.']),
 ('What it means for a shipper',['A faster, more reliable rail service for containers, which can mean shorter transit between the ports and the inland depots, and fewer delays on a line shared with passenger trains. Double-stacking improves the cost per container on long routes.','The benefit depends on whether the first and last mile are rail-connected and on the rail operator\'s schedule.']),
 ('What to ask',['Which terminal the container will leave from and arrive at, and whether it goes by the dedicated corridor or the older line.','The transit time and the cut-offs at the rail terminal, which are often earlier than for a road movement.','The charges and the free days at each stage. Ask your forwarder to compare rail and road for your cargo.'])],
 [('Is every container rail-moved on the freight corridor?','No. Only containers booked on a train that uses the corridor move this way. Others go by road or on the older rail network. Ask your forwarder what route is planned.')])
X('un-locode-and-customs-port-codes-india',[
 ('Reading the codes',['A UN/LOCODE has five characters: two letters for the country and three for the place. Indian ports in the system start with IN, such as INNSA for Nhava Sheva and INMUN for Mundra. Shipping lines, forwarders and customers use these codes on bookings and tracking, so everybody refers to the same place.','The customs location code is separate. It is a six-character code used on the customs bills, such as INNSA1 for Nhava Sheva port, INMUN1 for Mundra and INDEL4 for the Delhi air cargo complex. The last digit shows the type of location.']),
 ('Where you use them',['On the shipping bill and the bill of entry, which must show the right customs location.','On booking forms, the bill of lading and tracking requests with the line.','In freight quotes, where a code helps to avoid confusion between places with similar names.']),
 ('How to find one',['Use the code list on the customs portal for the customs location code, and the UN/LOCODE list for the place code. If you are not sure, ask your broker or forwarder. A wrong code can send a declaration to the wrong office and cause delay.'])],
 [('Are the UN/LOCODE and the customs code the same?','No. The UN/LOCODE identifies a place in a global list, while the customs location code is used inside the Indian customs system for filing the bills.')])
X('indian-ports-overview',[
 ('The main gateways',['On the west coast, the Mumbai region has Nhava Sheva (JNPT), the largest container port, with Mundra, Pipavav and Hazira in Gujarat and Kandla (Deendayal) nearby. In the south, Cochin and Mangalore are major ports, and Vizhinjam has been developed as a deep water port. On the east and south-east, Chennai, Kattupalli, Krishnapatnam, Visakhapatnam, Paradip and Kolkata with Haldia serve the other regions.']),
 ('Choosing a port',['Choose by the location of your factory or warehouse, the shipping lines and services that call at the port, the rail and road links, and the costs. For the northern states, Mundra and Nhava Sheva are linked to Delhi by rail, and many exporters in the north use them. For the south, Chennai, Tuticorin and Cochin are closer.','A port that is nearer is not always cheaper, since the services and the freight rates can differ.']),
 ('Other points to compare',['The weekly sailings to your destination, and whether the service is direct or needs a transhipment.','Congestion and the average dwell time for containers, which affect the cost.','The facilities for your cargo, such as reefer plugs, cranes for heavy lift or dangerous goods approvals.'])],
 [('Which is the busiest container port in India?','Nhava Sheva (JNPT) and Mundra are among the largest by volume, and their ranking can change from year to year. Check the latest figures if you need them.')])
